## Topology and the LAG problem
`show system pluggable` on the stack (08:39): every partner-cabled port is a copper SFP —
1.0.2 AT-SPTXa, 1.0.9 AT-SPTXc, 1.0.13 AT-SP10TM, 3.0.2 AT-SPTXa, 4.0.2 AT-SPTX, 4.0.9 AT-SP10T.
The IE520-28GSX has no fixed copper port. Aggregators: sa1={3.0.2,4.0.2}, sa2={1.0.2,1.0.9},
sa3={1.0.13,4.0.9}. STP is `no spanning-tree rstp enable` on every device (pre-test configs),
so a link freed from its LAG must be VLAN-isolated or it forms a stack↔x230 loop.

Run 1 (08:43:08–08:44:24, framework-run1-aborted/): only port1.0.9 freed (VLAN 3999). The frame
sorts non-LAG links first and binds `cusfp` BEFORE `copper`, so it bound
  role 'cusfp'  -> swi_a port1.0.9 <-> swi_f port1.0.4
  role 'copper' -> swi_a port1.0.2 [LAG member] <-> swi_f port1.0.3   + WARNING aggregator member
Stopped by SIGINT 3 s into configure(); device logs show only `show boot` — nothing saved.
Lesson: on this bench a tester must free TWO links for the copper role to land on a non-LAG port.

Run 2 binding (run.stdout 08:48:48):
  topology: role 'cusfp'  -> swi_a port1.0.2 <-> swi_f port1.0.3 (cusfp)
  topology: role 'copper' -> swi_a port1.0.9 <-> swi_f port1.0.4 (cusfp)
  topology: role 'tb'     -> swi_a port3.0.13 <-> tb eth1 (tb)
  (no [LAG member] tag, no WARNING)
Test link = stack member 1 port1.0.9 (AT-SPTXc, RJ45) ↔ x230-10GP port1.0.4 (fixed copper).

